<h1>TPlaceHolder</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TPlaceHolder" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TPlaceHolder</tt> reserves a spot in a template. It renders no element of its own, and
it has no style properties, because it extends <tt>TControl</tt> rather than
<tt>TWebControl</tt>. What it renders is its child controls, in the place where the
control sits.
</p>

<h2>Filling a placeholder</h2>

<p class="block-content">
A placeholder written with no body content marks a position that code fills later. A
handler adds controls to its <tt>Controls</tt> collection, and they render at that
position.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TPlaceHolder ID="Messages" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
$label = new TLabel();
$label-&gt;Text = 'Saved.';
$this-&gt;Messages-&gt;Controls[] = $label;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Showing and hiding a region</h2>

<p class="block-content">
A placeholder written with body content groups that content without adding markup around
it. Setting <tt>Visible</tt> to false then removes the whole group from the output. A
<a href="?page=Controls.Panel">TPanel</a> does the same, except that a panel renders a
<tt>&lt;div&gt;</tt> element around the group.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TPlaceHolder ID="AdminTools" Visible="false"&gt;
  &lt;com:TButton Text="Delete" OnClick="deleteItem" /&gt;
  &lt;com:TButton Text="Publish" OnClick="publishItem" /&gt;
&lt;/com:TPlaceHolder&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
A hidden control renders nothing, and PRADO skips its children as well. It still takes
part in the page lifecycle, so its state survives a postback.
</p>

